Z||000000||87ECO5||09-11-2000||09:32 AM||||Hmmm ... maybe I'll just keep that 4-banger instead of swapping in a thirsty V6 ...<P> <A HREF="http://my.aol.com/news/story.tmpl?table=n&cat=0180&id=0009110816254664" TARGET=_blank>http://my.aol.com/news/story.tmpl?table=n&cat=0180&id=0009110816254664</A> <P>Anyone else worried about us running out of oil?||||reg||9
Z||000001||Raydar||09-11-2000||10:38 AM||||Naaahhh. IMHO, we're not going to run out. Once imported oil gets expensive enough, it will once again be profitable for us to drill our own, on a large scale. And OPEC knows it. <BR>I believe that gas will stabilize just a bit higher than it is now. <BR>(Adjusted for inflation, we're still a good bit below where we were in 86.) <BR>Just my $.02.<BR> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||09-11-2000||11:44 AM||||I seen on the news about this issue also.  That the US is starting to pump more oil from our own wells.<P>This world is huge, and it does seem like we use a lot of oil.  But there is a lot of places that we havn't drilled yet.  I sometimes think about that we will eventually run out of oil. <P>Yes im still anti SUV.  SUVs are very popular and notice the price increase of gas while the suv sales increased?  Supply and demand.  Also the news did this thing on how SUVs use so much energy.<BR>I dont have all the exact data but its something like this<BR>using an suv is like <BR>leaving a bathroom light on for 28 years<BR>leaving your tv on for about the same time period||||reg||1
Z||000003||HugGRRRFiero||09-11-2000||01:02 PM||||I agree with your point on SUV's fierospeeder.  I bet many of those people who drive those things get dressed up for Earth Day every year.  Probably even the same people telling us to run out and hug some trees and save the rainforests.  Isn't that kind of hypocritical?<P>If Firestone tires were coming apart on our Fiero's, probably nobody would care...<P>"This post is a paid advertisment... These views are not necessarily the views of Pennock's Fiero Forum."||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||09-11-2000||01:41 PM||||I actually don't have much of a problem with SUVs, as far as gas consumption. The people who own 'em have to buy the gas, not me. They're not that terrible on gas, anyway.  <BR>Again, I don't think we're going to run out of oil. Every year, it seems like there is an announcement of the discovery of some vast new oil-field. In addition to that, there are new technologies constantly being developed that will enable more oil to be extracted from places that we've known about for years, but weren't previously able to tap. <P>Where I DO have a problem with SUVs, is the menace that their drivers create on the roads with their "I'm bigger than you, so I've got the right-of-way!" attitudes.<BR>But that's a different topic, altogether.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||09-11-2000||08:07 PM||||I think we'll have plenty of oil although I might not think so in 20 or 30 years.<P>Anyone else notice that OPEC is referred to as a "cartel".  Anyone else notice that large, illegal, rings of drug dealers are also referred to as "cartels".  Makes you wonder...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||09-11-2000||08:14 PM||||North America has a lot of untapped oil reserves. So much that when OPEC runs dry, the US and Canada will be in the position that OPEC is in now- following the "Golden Rule"- He who has the gold, makes the rules  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||deceler8||09-13-2000||02:24 PM||||The price of oil is very cyclical...remember when gas was last $1.50 per gallon...when the Shah was deposed in the late '70's/early '80's ? And was then 60 cents in 1986 ?<P>The world uses about 70 million barrels a day and a million barrel shortage or surplus over daily usage makes a huge difference in the price.<P>A lot of oil will come on line in the next five years as a lot of new technology improves efficiency of oil usage. Net effect of that will be $20-25 per barrel oil by 2005 (if not lower).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||frontal lobe||09-13-2000||04:27 PM||||Oh,oh.  Better get out the old "Road Warrior" video to start brushing up on what the world will be like in a few years when the oil runs out!  Gotta find me a mangy dog side-kick.  Don't think my current west highland terrior is going to be much help!<P>There is still a lot of oil left.  There are also a lot of other energy sources for the future--natural gas, ethanol (the cartel will be Iowa, Illinois, Indiana--grow that corn and ante up), coal, solar.  Only problem is none of them currently are as fun to power our Fiero's.  Hey, there is already one electric/solar Fiero!!<P>Bottom line:  don't be stupid and waste energy, but don't be stupid and not drive to the store when you want, and don't be really stupid and carpool with a load of non-bathing, non-shaving, non-armpit shaving, non-eyebrow plucking, tree-hugging, dolphin-saving (I like dolphins), spotted owl-protecting (although they've never seen one and never will) over-zealous wacko's either.  Let's see, did I leave anything out?  Oh yeah, don't go buy an orange 1974 Volvo 240 wagon and cover it with environmental cliche bumperstickers either.||||reg||1
Z||000009||frontal lobe||09-13-2000||04:29 PM||||Oh yeah, forgot one more thing while I was ranting.  My "thirstier" v-6 gets 25mpg mixed driving.  That's not whomping on it, but not babying it either.  So they can't blame our Fiero, evil sport cars for sucking up all the oil.  We get WAY better than all those SUV's that block out our sun when we are driving around them on the roadways.||||reg||1
